{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-99032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+24x+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+24x+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+7) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-99032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=-6$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=2$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=3$ and $x=5$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-21495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-21495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+9) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-30x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-21495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-21495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-21495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-4$ and $x=-1$"] ["text" "$x=-4$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=4$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-21495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ and $x=4$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-72x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-72x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x-72 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-131188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1