 and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-99019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=-5$"] ["text" "$x=-4$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2$"] ["text" "$x=\\frac{4}{3}$ and $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+24x-11$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+24x-11) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+18x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+18x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344544" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4})}^{′}{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){({e}^{4{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-131273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=3$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1}) \\\\  & ={((x+1)^{3})}^{′}{e}^{x-1}+(x+1)^{3}{({e}^{x-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x-1}+(x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1} \\\\  & =[3(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}]{e}^{x-1} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}(x+4){e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}(x+4){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-1}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-1}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344857" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-12x){e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-12x){e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=0$ and  $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"}