attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+6x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} \\\\  & =(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+6 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-3.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-131185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ only"] ["text" "$x=-9$ and $x=9$"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=9$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-131185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-3$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-1)}^{′}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1){({e}^{-{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1)(-2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}(1-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x(2-{x}^{2}){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-\\sqrt{2})(x+\\sqrt{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{2},\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ $x=0,$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344861" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-99056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ and $x=2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-48741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-2$ and $x=2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+12-4{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12-2x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-99053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=-6$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$"] ["text" "$x=-6$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-99053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-3$ and $x=2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+8-(2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-2x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So, $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-344867" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3-2{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3-2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-48922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-48922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-3$ and $x=1$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$\n\n- setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-314779" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-127391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-127391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-127391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-127391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-127391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{21{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-127391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-345502" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-132567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈1.292\\,50$ rounded to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-1.849\\,10$ rounded to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈1.292\\,50,\\,x≈-1.849\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.849\\,10$ and $x≈1.292\\,50.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}≈-1.849\\,10$ and ${x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.849\\,10\\cdot 1.292\\,50≈-2.39\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced3aa35b23e6a88e906296cc5bad7aea963275c20ee16363fad0cc5d7a589d1"} {"question" "q-132569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.02"] ["math" "-2.39"] ["math" "-2.78"] ["math" "-3.52"] ["math" "-3.98"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "q-132569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2.39"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/state.json" "file_sha256" "086c9a35cfa0acb6ecc8d665368882a985dbdf3b604446becf7c0ae2d3e974ac"} {"question" "e-6722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2+5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-6722.json" "file_sha256" "7308399cd59d0b1f9bf7d2bfc9c2a98bf6f1fcdec3f76ea4c5909174685b1542"} {"question" "e-6722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=0,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-6722.json" "file_sha256" "7308399cd59d0b1f9bf7d2bfc9c2a98bf6f1fcdec3f76ea4c5909174685b1542"} {"question" "e-6723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = e^{x^3-x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-6723.json" "file_sha256" "de564363cfd0ff79fd1696e25b5ad93568aebfc342f12ac7cd4439ba61b07559"} {"question" "e-6723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{x^3-x} \\neq 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by $e^{x^3-x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-6723.json" "file_sha256" "de564363cfd0ff79fd1696e25b5ad93568aebfc342f12ac7cd4439ba61b07559"} {"question" "e-8757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac {x-3}{x^2 -6}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-8757.json" "file_sha256" "d1fdd54e3ead5046e7956371bc6f93d0e360cc6d4f9a8ef243546c659a5e80c7"} {"question" "e-8757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt 6.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-8757.json" "file_sha256" "d1fdd54e3ead5046e7956371bc6f93d0e360cc6d4f9a8ef243546c659a5e80c7"} {"question" "e-9576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}.$ Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-9576.json" "file_sha256" "469549687d85782ee5dc161f5de8c15e3ed486dc4e687d2878883cfb6c033f22"} {"question" "e-9576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\nx =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980155/example-9576.json" "file_sha256" "469549687d85782ee5dc161f5de8c15e3ed486dc4e687d2878883cfb6c033f22"}] "usage" {"e-8757" [] "q-48741" [] "q-21495" [] "e-9576" [] "e-6723" [] "e-6722" []} "basis" 865 "retractions" [[17592186083524 :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = 2x^{3} - 15x^{2} + 24x + 9$."] [17592186083641 :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}$?"] [17592186082989 :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2+5$."] [17592186082989 :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 5) \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} - 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$6x^{2} - 6x|= 0; 6x(x - 1)|= 0; x = 0, 1$\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=1$."] [17592186082990 :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = e^{x^3-x}$."] [17592186082990 :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x^{3} - x}) \\\\\n&= (x^{3} - x)^{′}e^{x^{3} - x} \\\\\n&= (3x^{2} - 1)e^{x^{3} - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$(3x^{2} - 1)e^{x^{3} - x}|= 0$\nSince $e^{x^3-x} \\neq 0$ for all $x$, we can divide this equation by $e^{x^3-x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n$(3x^{2} - 1)e^{x^{3} - x}|= 0; 3x^{2} - 1|= 0; x|= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$"] [17592186083044 :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac {x-3}{x^2 -6}$?"] [17592186083044 :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x - 3}{x^{2} - 6}) \\\\\n&= ((x - 3)^{′} \\cdot (x^{2} - 6) - (x - 3) \\cdot (x^{2} - 6)^{′})/((x^{2} - 6)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((x^{2} - 6) - (x - 3) \\cdot 2x)/((x^{2} - 6)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (-6 + 6x - x^{2})/((x^{2} - 6)^{2}) \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} - 6x + 6)/((x^{2} - 6)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n$- (x^{2} - 6x + 6)/((x^{2} - 6)^{2})|= 0; x^{2} - 6x + 6|= 0; x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 6|= 0; (x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 6|= 0; (x - 3)^{2} - 3|= 0; (x - 3)^{2}|= 3; x - 3|= ± \\sqrt{3}; x|= 3 ± \\sqrt{3}$.\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt 6$. But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=3\\pm \\sqrt 3\n$$"] [17592186083051 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}$. Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$."] [17592186083051 :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{3}\\sin 2x - \\cos 2x) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin 2x) - \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x|= 0; 2\\sin 2x|=-2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x; \\frac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}|=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos 2x}{\\cos 2x}; \\tan 2x|=-\\sqrt{3}$\nThe general solution to our equation is\n$2x|= \\arctan (-\\sqrt{3}) + nπ; 2x|=-\\frac{π}{3} + nπ$.\nSolving for $x$, we get\n$x|=-\\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{nπ}{2}$.\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$, until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$:setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$All other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are\n$x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186080638 17592186080639 17592186080640 17592186080641 17592186080642 17592186081213 17592186081214 17592186081215 17592186081216 17592186081217] "immutable_fields" [[17592186080637 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080637 :answer-field/id] [17592186080637 :answer-field/key] [17592186080637 :answer-field/type] [17592186081212 :answer-field/correct] [17592186081212 :answer-field/id] [17592186081212 :answer-field/key] [17592186081212 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
